    "final_message": "The red acceptance test now passes, and its file is byte-identical to the red commit (e97d910). The full gate is green: `RESTATE_INGRESS=http://localhost:38480 RESTATE_ADMIN=http://localhost:39470 STORY_DRAFTING_PORT=9340 scripts/verify story-drafting` gives tessercheck zero findings, `mypy --strict` clean, 85 tests passing in the non-root run and the root tier passing against the served host. I didn't need to change `scripts/verify`, and I added no `skip` lines. The work is committed on `baseline/runs/green-v3-1` as `948e825`, not pushed.\n\n**What I built** (all in `story-drafting/drafting/`):\n- **Opening a run no longer blocks.** The run service saves the new run as `drafting` with no stories, then starts the `RunOrchestrator` workflow without waiting for it (`start_draft_stories`). So `POST /runs` answers 201 straight away, and the first `GET` already finds the run. It has to start rather than wait, because the workflow now never finishes on its own.\n- **Drafting sits behind a port with one deterministic adapter.** The port is `StoryWriter`, in `application/ports/story_writer.py`. Its only adapter, `TemplateStoryWriter` in `adapters/gateways/`, fills three fixed templates with the job. A new `StoryActions` action and its activity, registered in a new `StoryActions` Restate container, call it. I added that container to the component and to the host's Restate app.\n- **The `Run` aggregate has a lifecycle.** A `draft(Stories)` transition moves it from `drafting` to `awaiting_pick`. It refuses an empty draft and a second draft. `Story` and `Stories` are new value objects, and `RunStatus` is a closed set of states.\n- **The workflow order is:** write the stories, put them on the `Run`, record the run through `RunActions`, then wait on the `pick_story` durable promise.\n- **The read path carries the new fields.** `GET /runs/{run_id}` now also answers `status` and `stories`, via the port, client and handler types and the memory store.\n\n**Left out as out of scope:** nothing "
  },
